<Doc>
    <Section title="Every value is a `Reactive`">
        <Example
            id="one-type"
            app="crm"
            title="`Reactive` \u2014 four ways to get a value, one way to ask"
            summary="A note on a contact. Watch which rows say \u2026 on arrival, then type a note and save it."
            files={{
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactNote.abide': contactNoteSource,
                'src/contacts/lib/ContactNote.test.ts': contactNoteTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ContactNote />
        </Example>

        <p>A value the page owns, a value derived from it, a value loaded from a handler, and a
            value that waits for its first write: all four come from <code>reactive</code>, and all
            four are a <code>Reactive</code>. <code>ready()</code> asks each one the same question, so
            the branch you write for "not here yet" works on every one of them.</p>

        <p>Swapping a local value for a loaded one changes how it is declared and nothing that
            reads it.</p>

        <p>A <code>Reactive</code> can also be awaited, so code that takes a promise for a load asks
            <code>isReactive</code> first. A <code>Reactive</code> is a value, even while it loads.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A transform shapes what the record keeps">
        <Example
            id="transform"
            app="crm"
            title={'`reactive(v, { transform })` — the record keeps the digits'}
            summary="A phone number typed with punctuation. Type one in the usual way."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/PhoneField.abide': phoneFieldSource,
                'src/profile/lib/PhoneField.test.ts': phoneFieldTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <PhoneField />
        </Example>

        <p>The transform runs on every write, so the field shows back what was stored rather than
            what you typed. <code>Accepted</code> is the type a write takes and <code>Stored</code> is
            the type the transform returns. <code>Shaped</code> is everything a transform can return,
            a refusal included, and <code>Transformer</code> is the type of the option.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A derived value follows the fields it read">
        <Example
            id="derived"
            app="crm"
            title="`reactive(() => …)` — initials and a count, computed from the fields"
            summary="A profile's initials and how much of it is filled in. Change the name, then add a phone."
            files={{
                'src/profile/lib/ProfileSummary.abide': profileSummarySource,
                'src/profile/lib/ProfileSummary.test.ts': profileSummaryTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <ProfileSummary />
        </Example>

        <p>Give <code>reactive</code> a body, <code>reactive(() =&gt; …)</code>, and what the body
            read on its last run is what reruns it. You list no dependencies. The test counts the work:
            a phone edit walks to the count and never reaches the initials, because the initials never
            read the phone.</p>

        <p>A body with a parameter, <code>reactive((args) =&gt; …)</code>, is a
            <code>Keyed</code>: a factory holding one value per set of arguments.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A refresh keeps the figures on screen">
        <Example
            id="pipeline"
            app="crm"
            title="`busy()` and `ready()` — a first load and a refresh, told apart"
            summary="The pipeline's total. Wait for it, then press Refresh: the old figure stays until the new one lands."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/Pipeline.abide': pipelineSource,
                'src/deals/listDeals.handler.ts': listDealsSource,
                'src/deals/lib/Pipeline.test.ts': pipelineTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Pipeline />
        </Example>

        <p>A first load is <code>busy()</code> with nothing <code>ready()</code>. A refresh is
            <code>busy()</code> and still <code>ready()</code>, so the tile keeps the figure it has
            while the new one loads. One loading flag cannot tell the two apart, and blanks the page on
            every refresh. <code>refresh(deals)</code> loads the value again.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Probe</th><th>Answers</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.busy</code></td><td>whether work is in flight for the value</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.ready</code></td><td>whether there is a value to show</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.error</code></td><td>the standing failure, handed back rather than thrown</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.issues</code></td><td>the issues of a standing validation refusal, field by field</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>busy</code></td><td>whether anything in a selection is in flight: an app-wide progress bar</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ready</code></td><td>whether everything in a selection has a value</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>A probe never throws and never starts a load, so it is safe to read before anything has
            landed. A read of the value itself outside the markup, in a click handler, throws
            <code>NotLanded</code> until it lands. The value can also be awaited, for a handler or a test: <code>await deals</code>
            waits for it, and <code>s.then</code>, <code>s.catch</code> and <code>s.finally</code> reach the same
            settling.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="The form clears once the deal is added">
        <Example
            id="quick-deal"
            app="crm"
            title={'`saving.then(…)` — the next step, once the call lands'}
            summary="Sales adds renewals one after another. Press Add deal: the name clears when the server has the deal, ready for the next."
            files={{
                'src/deals/lib/QuickDeal.abide': quickDealSource,
                'src/deals/createDeal.handler.ts': createDealSource,
                'src/deals/lib/QuickDeal.test.ts': quickDealTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <QuickDeal />
        </Example>

        <p>The kept call can be awaited like a promise, so what comes after a save is
            <code>saving.then(…)</code>: clear a field, close a dialog, go to the new deal's page. It
            runs once the call lands, and the call still runs once. A refusal is already on
            <code>saving.error()</code>, so a <code>then</code> given one function does not report it
            as unhandled; an <code>await</code> still throws it. A <code>then</code> made before
            another press is answered by the call the form keeps when it settles, which is the later
            one.</p>

        <p><code>then</code> hands back a reactive value of its own rather than a promise: busy until
            the next step ends, holding what that step answered, and failing with whichever step
            failed — a refusal passed through with its <code>issues()</code>, or a throw from the
            step. A step that answers another call is followed until that call lands. Left unread,
            it is a promise nobody handled, and a throw from the step is reported; kept or read, the
            throw is on its <code>error()</code> instead.</p>

        <p>The answer is the call's, so the next call drops it until it lands. To keep the last
            answer on screen through a call in flight or a refusal, write it into the value it is about:
            <code>{'saving.then((moved) => (deal = moved))'}</code> writes the answer over a derived
            <code>deal</code>, which keeps it until the next answer lands.</p>

        <p>A form that moves on once it is saved — to the new deal's page, or back to the list —
            is pressed once: <code>{'disabled={saving.busy() || saving.ready()}'}</code>.
            <code>ready()</code> holds from the answer until the next call is kept, so the button
            stays off while the page moves, and a refusal turns it back on. A label that reads
            <code>busy()</code> stays on through the next step when the form keeps what
            <code>then</code> hands back, answering what the call did:
            <code>{'saving = createDeal(…).then(async (made) => { await navigate(…); return made })'}</code>.</p>

        <p>To let go of a call, keep one nothing has made: <code>saving = reactive&lt;typeof
                createDeal&gt;()</code> leaves no refusal standing and nothing in flight, for a dialog
            opened again over the last one's error. <code>saving = undefined</code> is not the same:
            it writes <code>undefined</code> over the answer of the call being kept.</p>

        <p>One value can keep a call of any of several handlers, for a flow with one busy flag and
            one error: <code>reactive&lt;typeof moveDeal | typeof createDeal&gt;()</code> keeps
            either, with the failures of both. Its <code>then</code> is handed either answer, so a
            step that needs the new deal's <code>id</code> keeps the call under its own name first:
            <code>const made = createDeal(…)</code>, <code>saving = made</code>, then
            <code>made.then(…)</code>, typed as that call alone.</p>

        <p>A step that is not a call goes under the same busy flag and error by keeping the promise
            that ends in the call: <code>{'saving = file.text().then((csv) => importDeals({ csv }))'}</code>
            is busy from reading the file to the server's answer, and its error is whichever step
            failed.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A call with the same arguments is one load">
        <Example
            id="stage-shared"
            app="crm"
            title={'`getStage({ stage })` — one entry per set of arguments'}
            summary="Three panels, each with its own stage picker. Put one on won, and then the others."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTiles.abide': stageTilesSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageCount.abide': stageCountSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageValue.abide': stageValueSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageAverage.abide': stageAverageSource,
                'src/pipeline/getStage.handler.ts': getStageSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTiles.test.ts': stageTilesTestSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/StageTiles.bench.ts': stageTilesBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <StageTiles />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench puts all three panels on one stage once each entry has been answered, among 100
            deals and among 1,000. Its ratio is what a pick costs as the pipeline grows: the panels
            read the entry the server summed, and never the deals.</p>

        <p>The three panels never speak to each other, and nothing is lifted into a parent. When
            they ask for the same stage they get the same entry, so the later panels paint at once,
            with no request. On two stages they are two entries, and each
            pays for its own.</p>

        <p>A body with a parameter is a keyed value: one entry per set of arguments, held. That is
            true of a handler like <code>getStage</code>, and of <code>{'reactive((args) => …)'}</code>
            written in a page. The arguments are its <code>Args</code>, and the key is their JSON with
            the properties sorted, so argument order never splits an entry.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>reactive</code> body, keyed</td><td><code>{'reactive(({ stage }) => …)'}</code> holds one entry per stage. Its body does not track what it reads; triggers reload it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>k.busy</code>, <code>k.ready</code></td><td>whether any entry of a keyed value, or those a partial <code>Args</code> matches, is loading or has landed</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>rpc.busy</code>, <code>rpc.ready</code></td><td>the same two on a handler: <code>{"getStage.busy({ stage: 'won' })"}</code></td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>global</code></td><td><code>{'{ global: true }'}</code> shares one cache across every caller in the process, where the default is one per request on a server and one per tab in a browser</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`refresh` reloads now, and `invalidate` marks stale">
        <Example
            id="stale"
            app="crm"
            title="`refresh()` and `invalidate()` — the two ways back to the server"
            summary="The weighted forecast, with both buttons under it. Press Reload now, then Mark stale."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/Forecast.abide': forecastSource,
                'src/pipeline/getForecast.handler.ts': getForecastSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/Forecast.test.ts': forecastTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <Forecast />
        </Example>

        <p><code>refresh()</code> loads now and keeps the forecast on screen while it does: the
            value is <code>busy()</code> and still <code>ready()</code>, and it swaps when the new one
            lands. <code>invalidate()</code> loads nothing. It marks what is held as wrong, and the
            next read fetches.</p>

        <p>Reach for one or the other, not both. A reload over a value marked stale has nothing
            trusted to serve, so the figure blanks until it lands.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>s.refresh</code></td><td>reloads one value now, serving what is held until the new one lands</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>s.invalidate</code></td><td>marks one value stale; the next read loads</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>ttl</code></td><td><code>{'{ ttl: 60_000 }'}</code> lets a value expire a minute after it loaded. Expiry wakes nobody, and the next read loads. On a value with nothing to load from, it is how long a write stands</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>poll</code></td><td><code>{'{ poll: 60_000 }'}</code> refreshes a value every minute for as long as the component or request that built it stands, skipping a minute its load is still running</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A confirmation goes back to how it started">
        <Example
            id="ttl-reset"
            app="crm"
            title={'`reactive(false, { ttl: 2_000 })` — a flag that falls back on its own'}
            summary="A deal with a button that copies its link. Copy it, and watch the button."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealLink.abide': dealLinkSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealLink.test.ts': dealLinkTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealLink />
        </Example>

        <p><code>copied</code> has no handler and no body behind it, so its <code>ttl</code> has
            nothing to load. Instead, two seconds after a write, the value is written back to what it
            was built with (6.2). There is no <code>setTimeout</code> to clear: a second copy starts
            the two seconds over (6.11), and the timer stops with the component (6.14), so a link
            copied from a panel that then closes writes nothing afterwards.</p>

        <p>What is written back is a fresh copy of the value as it was built (6.15), so a
            <code>patch</code> on an opening list or record never changes what comes back. That
            makes the opening value plain data: one a copy cannot stand for, such as a class
            instance, is refused where the value is built (6.16).</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A search box waits for the typing to stop">
        <Example
            id="search"
            app="crm"
            title={'`{ debounce }` — one search once the typing stops'}
            summary="A search over the deals. Type hopper quickly, and watch the results rather than the field."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealSearch.abide': dealSearchSource,
                'src/pipeline/searchDeals.handler.ts': searchDealsSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealSearch.test.ts': dealSearchTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealSearch />
        </Example>

        <p>The debounce is on the query, not on the search. <code>q</code> takes each keystroke and
            changes once the keys stop for 300 ms, and <code>found</code> is a derived value that calls
            <code>searchDeals</code> with it, so it runs only when <code>q</code> changes: six
            keystrokes are one request, with no debounced function to write. The results already held
            stay on screen meanwhile, and the test counts the searches to prove it.</p>

        <p>The same option on <code>found</code> would hold back its run instead (5.11). Put it on
            the value the typing writes where several things read it, so they all wait for the same
            pause.</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>What it does for the pipeline</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>debounce</code></td><td>changes once the changes stop for that many ms</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>throttle</code></td><td>changes at once, then at most once per window: a live deal count that updates every second, not every message</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>identity</code></td><td>what counts as the same value: a new value equal to the held one by it wakes nobody. The default is the reference</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>structural</code></td><td><code>{'{ identity: structural }'}</code> compares by content, so a stage whose <code>{'{ count, value }'}</code> reloads unchanged does not repaint</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>transform</code></td><td><code>{'{ transform: (names) => names.toSorted() }'}</code> reshapes the results once, as they land, rather than in every reader. The function is a <code>Transformer</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Section title="`patch` changes one row of a list in place">
        <Example
            id="patch"
            app="crm"
            title="`deals.patch(mutate)` — change what is held, and wake its readers"
            summary="The deal list, loaded once. Updates arrive one deal at a time; apply a few."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealFeed.abide': dealFeedSource,
                'src/pipeline/listDeals.handler.ts': pipelineListDealsSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealFeed.test.ts': dealFeedTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealFeed />
        </Example>

        <p><code>patch</code> hands you the value already held, lets you change it, and wakes what
            reads it. One update rewrites one amount: no list refetched, no row rebuilt. The test
            counts one text write and no nodes made or moved.</p>

        <p>The <code>by deal.id</code> on the loop keeps each row tied to its deal, so the row
            the update names is the only one that changes.</p>

        <p><code>patch</code> changes the array it holds and copies nothing. A path write in a
            <code>.abide</code> file, <code>{'deals[0].amount = 27_500'}</code>, also wakes the
            readers, but copies the path it writes down first. In a <code>.ts</code> file, or where
            the held value is not plain data, <code>patch</code> is the write that wakes them.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Two components far apart share one value">
        <Example
            id="share"
            app="crm"
            title="`reactive.share(key, build)` — one value for a subtree"
            summary="A detail panel above a row of deals. Pick a deal, and the panel follows."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealPicker.abide': dealPickerSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealRow.abide': dealRowSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealDetail.abide': dealDetailSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/DealPicker.test.ts': dealPickerTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealPicker />
        </Example>

        <p><code>DealPicker</code> creates <code>selectedDeal</code>. Every row and the detail
            panel ask for the same key, find that one on the way up, and never run their own build.
            No prop runs between them.</p>

        <p>A value lives in
            the component that created it and below, so two pickers on one page each get their own.
            Where a value is one thing for the whole app, export it from a module instead.</p>

        <p><code>Shared</code> is an interface you widen with declaration merging to type each
            key. It is optional: without it, the type comes from the build function.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A `store` keeps a value between visits">
        <Example
            id="store"
            app="crm"
            title={'`{ store }` — where a value lives between visits'}
            summary="The quarter's target, which the whole team works to. Set a new one, then reload the page."
            files={{
                'src/pipeline/lib/TeamTarget.abide': teamTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/targetStore.ts': targetStoreSource,
                'src/pipeline/getTarget.handler.ts': getTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/setTarget.handler.ts': setTargetSource,
                'src/pipeline/lib/TeamTarget.test.ts': teamTargetTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <TeamTarget />
        </Example>

        <p>A <code>Store</code> is a <code>get</code> and a <code>set</code>. The value reads the
            store when it is built and falls back to <code>500_000</code> on a miss without writing it,
            and every change is written back. The reads and writes in the markup do not change.</p>

        <p>This store goes through two handlers, so the target is one value on the server: the
            next visit opens on it, and so does everyone else's, painted right the first time. After
            a write, <code>set</code> invalidates the read it made, so the next read asks the server
            rather than this tab's copy. A setting one visitor alone sees, such as a theme, belongs
            in a cookie instead.</p>

        <p>Where the stored value can change from outside the app, such as a file another program
            edits, the store adds <code>watch</code>. It is handed a function to call when that
            happens, and returns the function that stops watching. The value reads <code>get</code>
            again, and the body does not run.</p>

        <p>Five stores come with abide, the members of <code>store</code>, for the places a value most often lives:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Where the value lives</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td><code>store.web</code></td><td><code>{"store.web('layout')"}</code> keeps it in this browser's <code>localStorage</code>, or <code>sessionStorage</code> with <code>{"{ area: 'session' }"}</code>, and follows a write another tab makes. On the server it is never there, so the page renders the fallback</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>store.sqlite</code></td><td><code>{"store.sqlite(crmDatabase, 'target')"}</code>, on <code>@abide/abide/server</code>'s <code>store</code> alone, keeps it in a SQLite table of the file a <code>database</code> opener opens, so a restarted server opens on it</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>store.redis</code></td><td><code>{"store.redis(Bun.redis, 'target')"}</code>, on <code>@abide/abide/server</code>'s <code>store</code> alone, keeps it in a Redis key that Redis expires with the <code>ttl</code>, so every server behind one Redis opens on it. Where Redis reports changes to its keys, it follows a write another server makes</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>store.indexedDB</code></td><td><code>{"store.indexedDB('draft')"}</code> keeps it in this browser's IndexedDB, for a value too large for <code>localStorage</code>, and follows a write another tab makes. On the server it is never there, so the page renders the fallback</td></tr>
                <tr><td><code>store.cookies</code></td><td><code>{"store.cookies('theme')"}</code> keeps it in a cookie, which the browser sends with every request, so the server renders the stored value rather than the fallback. A cookie holds 4 KB at most</td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>Each keeps the value under the app's name and then the key, so two apps sharing one origin
            or one Redis never read each other's. Each keeps it as JSON with the value's
            <code>ttl</code> beside it, and passes what it reads through a <code>schema</code> when
            given one: a restore skips the value's own, so an entry an older build wrote is a miss
            rather than a wrong value.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel is an array anything can append to">
        <Example
            id="channel"
            app="crm"
            title="`reactive<Activity[]>([])` — a channel anything can reach"
            summary="A quick-log bar on a contact, and a line above it that reports the last touch. Log a call and watch a component the bar never names."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/activity.ts': activitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/QuickLog.abide': quickLogSource,
                'src/activity/lib/LastActivity.abide': lastActivitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/QuickLog.test.ts': quickLogTestSource,
                'src/activity/lib/QuickLog.bench.ts': quickLogBenchSource,
            }}
        >
            <QuickLog />
        </Example>

        <p>The bench logs a call to a channel of 100 entries, and of 1,000, letting the oldest go
            each time. Its ratio is what a log costs as the channel grows: the patch changes the
            entries in place, and the channel is never copied.</p>

        <p><code>activity.ts</code> holds the channel and the one function that writes it. The bar
            imports the function, the readout imports the channel, and neither imports the other. No
            prop is threaded between them and no provider wraps them.</p>

        <p>The readout derives <code>last</code> as <code>activity.at(-1)</code>, and follows the
            channel the way any derived value follows what it read. Opened with an array, the channel
            has landed: it is <code>ready()</code> at once and never <code>busy()</code> where it is
            written.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="A channel keeps its last few entries">
        <Example
            id="recent"
            app="crm"
            title="`patch` with a trim — the last three entries"
            summary="The same channel listed in full, already three entries long. Log a meeting and watch the top of the list."
            files={{
                'src/activity/lib/RecentActivity.abide': recentActivitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/activity.ts': activitySource,
                'src/activity/lib/RecentActivity.test.ts': recentActivityTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <RecentActivity />
        </Example>

        <p>The trim is one line in the patch that appends: <code>if (entries.length &gt; 3)
                entries.shift()</code>. <code>patch</code> hands you the array the channel holds and
            changes it in place, so the push and the trim are one write. abide puts no cap on the
            array. The condition is yours: a count here, an age or a byte total where that is what
            bounds it. Keep the push and the trim in one function every writer calls, and the cap
            belongs to the channel rather than to whichever caller remembered it.</p>

        <p>A value keeps no past of its own. Where you want what came before, make the value the
            array and append where you write, as here. An undo stack is the same: an array the app
            pushes into.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Background work is a value on a clock">
        <Example
            id="job"
            app="crm"
            title={'`reactive(async function* () { … }, { global: true, poll })` — a job'}
            summary="Every five minutes the CRM walks the leads and lists the ones still waiting on a follow-up. The list is here before anyone asks for it."
            files={{
                'src/ops/lib/FollowUps.abide': followUpsSource,
                'src/ops/lib/followUps.ts': followUpsJobSource,
                'src/ops/getFollowUps.handler.ts': getFollowUpsSource,
                'src/ops/lib/FollowUps.test.ts': followUpsTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <FollowUps />
        </Example>

        <p>A job is a reactive value: a body, <code>global</code> so the process has one of it, and a
            <code>poll</code> for the clock. Its first run is when the app starts serving, after
            every <code>onStart</code> has run, or when something reads it first; the value holds
            each lead it has yielded. A test that boots the app and never reads the job never runs
            it. Nothing else schedules it, so there is no <code>setInterval</code> in
            <code>onStart</code> to remember and no <code>clearInterval</code> in
            <code>onStop</code>.</p>

        <p>One run happens at a time: the clock skips a tick while <code>followUps.busy()</code>
            is true. <code>followUps.refresh()</code> runs it now. A run that throws is written to
            <code>abide:reactive</code> under the value's name, and the next tick runs it again.
            The app's stop ends the clock and waits for the run in flight before
            <code>onStop</code>, inside the same <code>ABIDE_STOP_TIMEOUT</code> as the requests.</p>

        <p>A number is a clock that ticks from the first run, whatever the runs take. A job whose
            run is long spells <code>{"poll: { every, after: 'settle' }"}</code> instead, and the next
            run starts <code>every</code> milliseconds after the last one ended. <code>delay</code>
            puts the first run off once the app starts serving. A function there is called when
            the app starts, so the wait can come from something outside the process, such as when a
            file was last written. A read still runs the job at once.</p>

        <p>A run that should not be waited on reads <code>stopSignal()</code>. The stop aborts it
            before waiting, so the run ends early and the stop waits only for that. A run that
            gives up by throwing the abort has not failed, and nothing is logged for it. A job that is
            switched off by configuration checks <code>config()</code> at the top of its body and
            returns.</p>
    </Section>

    <Section title="Heavy work runs in a worker thread">
        <Example
            id="worker"
            app="crm"
            title={'`new Worker(url)` behind `reactive(async function* () { … })` — work off the main thread'}
            summary="Scoring every deal takes long enough to stall every request the server is answering. It runs in a worker, and the scores arrive one deal at a time."
            files={{
                'src/ops/lib/DealScores.abide': dealScoresSource,
                'src/ops/lib/dealScores.ts': dealScoresValueSource,
                'src/ops/lib/scoreWorker.ts': scoreWorkerSource,
                'src/ops/getDealScores.handler.ts': getDealScoresSource,
                'src/ops/lib/DealScores.test.ts': dealScoresTestSource,
            }}
        >
            <DealScores />
        </Example>

        <p>A loop that runs for seconds holds the server's one thread, and no request is answered
            until it ends. A Bun <code>Worker</code> runs it on a thread of its own. The generator
            wrapping it is an ordinary reactive value: <code>busy()</code> while the worker scores,
            a score per message, and a throw in the worker refuses the value. The test answers a
            request while the scores are still coming, which the same loop run inline cannot do.</p>

        <p>This is a plain Bun <code>Worker</code>, and abide does not manage it. You own its
            lifetime:</p>

        <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Yours to decide</th><th>What this card does</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
                <tr><td>how many workers</td><td>one per run, ended in the generator's <code>finally</code>, which also runs when a <code>refresh()</code> discards the run in flight</td></tr>
                <tr><td>whether it keeps the process up</td><td><code>{'{ ref: false }'}</code>, so a run in flight never keeps the process alive</td></tr>
                <tr><td>when it runs</td><td>as a job, an hour after the last scoring ended, with <code>{"after: 'settle'"}</code>, and first a minute after the app starts serving, with <code>delay</code>. A test that never reads the scores never starts a worker</td></tr>
                <tr><td>what happens at stop</td><td><code>stopSignal()</code> aborts, the generator returns, and its <code>finally</code> ends the worker. The stop then waits only for that</td></tr>
                <tr><td>what it can read</td><td>only what it is sent. A worker loads its own copy of every module, so the deals go in by <code>postMessage</code></td></tr>
            </tbody>
        </table>
    </Section>

    <Registry />
</Doc>
